ivity and that chart  that we saw will will start to tick up  but chanter  is there a big elephant in the room here  i mean  you're saying all of this and you're  offering the money and so on  is one of the reasons that nothing  changes is that in spite of all this  money for training  fi the apprenticeship levy huge 2.7  billion i think uh  5 000 employers don't use it 400 million  worth of that levy  expired we understand last year and  isn't the problem really the one that  the prime minister and also your  colleague the home secretary frequently  talk about which is that as long as  employers can bring in cheap  labor through immigration they aren't  going to invest in training and skills  and raise productivity in the way that  you want it to be raised  well i wouldn't say that nothing has  changed and actually i think some of the  things that we've already put in place  are starting to yield benefits  that that graph is over the last 10  years but let me just look at the time  that i've had this job and what we're  seeing now what we're seeing is record  numbers of job vacancies we're seeing  employers wanting to hire we're seeing  businesses intentions to invest growing  again partly aided by measures that  we've taken like the super deduction  which is a huge tax incentive for  businesses to invest in increasing their  productivity and we're seeing people's  wages rise uh real wages today are three  and a half percent higher than they were  before the crisis so actually all the  things that we put in place the plan for  jobs that i've been on this show talking  about a lot over the last year and a  half that plan for jobs is working  people are seeing the benefits of it and  i'm confident that if we continue to  deliver on investing and infrastructure  innovation our skills we will see our  growth rise and next week's budget will  demonstrate that plan in action we will  build that stronger economy we'll drive  up our economic growth and productivity  and people are going to feel that in  higher living standards and a better  quality of life all right let's let's  talk about what some of your colleagues  in the conservative party say which is  that you're not really a very  conservative chancellor at all um  you said in  the uh the prime minister said in your  pa in the party conference that um you  would be first government in decades not  to dock these hard decisions you'd be  radical and optimistic let's see if  that's actually true  for example nobody wants to keep the  system of business rates  you said in your 2019 uh  manifesto that we will cut the burden of  tax on business by reducing business  rates and you promised a fundamental  review two years now are you going to be  telling us on wednesday that you're  going to get rid of business right well  i will talk on on wednesday about the  conclusion of that fundamental review so  it wouldn't be appropriate for me to do  that it's actually concluded so yes we  will get an outcome from this we'll get  an outcome from the review we've said  that we would conclude the review in the  autumn and you'll see that i mean but on  business rates it's important to  remember this is a government that's put  in place the small business rates  reduction which means a third of all  properties don't pay any business rates  at all any more and over the past year  and a half through chronovirus we've  delivered about 16 billion pounds worth  of tax cuts on business rates to help  hospitality and retail businesses get  through all of this and  in terms of the review and  i think you've got the shadow chancellor  on uh later on in the show i mean there  are people who say you should just  abolish business rates which i think is  what the labour party position is  business rates obviously  yeah business rates obviously does raise  25 billion pounds i'm sure you'll be  asking her well what is a system that  would replace that if you are going to  just stand up and say you're going to